 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Yes. On advertised standard variable rates, investors pay more than owner-occupiers. The data is from the RBA's indicator lending rates (table F5).\n\n**September 2026 (latest):**\n- Investor: **9.35%**\n- Owner-occupier: **8.77%**\n- Gap: **0.58 percentage points**\n\nThe gap has been the same 0.58 points in every month since January 2025. Both rates have moved together:\n- Both fell through 2025. Investor rates went from 9.35% to 8.60%, and owner-occupier rates from 8.77% to 8.02%.\n- Both held steady from August 2025 to January 2026.\n- Both rose from February to May 2026, and have been flat since.\n\nThese are advertised rates, not what borrowers actually pay. Most borrowers get discounts, and the rates actually paid on new loans are lower (RBA table F6). The investor premium on discounted or actual rates may be different from the standard-rate gap.",
